  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Oklahoma has received $3.8B in federal contract obligations in FY2026 to date. The state recorded 16,123 awards with an average award size of $234K. Obligations are down 44.5% year over year. Fiscal-year totals show a peak in FY25 at $6.8B and a decline to $3.8B in FY26. The Department of Defense is the largest buyer, obligating $2.5B so far this year. Aircraft Manufacturing leads industry spending with $681M, followed by Engineering Services with $354M and Other Support Activities for Air Transportation with $302M. THE BOEING COMPANY is the largest vendor by obligations at $789M, with a second Boeing listing at $150M and MCKESSON CORPORATION at $113M. The data reflect partial, fiscal year to date totals for FY2026; year-to-date figures are not full-year totals. Contractors and agencies tracking procurement activity should note the concentration of spending in defense-related agencies, aerospace and engineering industries, and a small group of large vendors accounting for substantial shares of obligations.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How has federal contracting in Oklahoma changed year over year?",
        "body": "Obligations are down 44.5% year over year. Fiscal-year totals rose from $4.5B in FY21 to $6.8B in FY25 and then fell to $3.8B in FY26. The Department of Defense remains the dominant buyer with $2.5B in obligations, which drives much of the year-to-date pattern."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Which sectors and vendors dominate federal spending in Oklahoma?",
        "body": "Aircraft Manufacturing is the largest industry with $681M in obligations, followed by Engineering Services at $354M and Other Support Activities for Air Transportation at $302M. THE BOEING COMPANY leads vendors with $789M, followed by a separate Boeing entry at $150M and MCKESSON CORPORATION at $113M."
      }
    ]
  },
